# 13. CINEMA LIGHTING AUTOMATION

Create a dedicated lighting controller.

Lighting must change automatically according to show state.

Implement the following four states.

## STATE 1 — AUDITORIUM OPEN

Before the show starts:

- Main lights ON.
- Ceiling lights ON.
- Wall lights ON.
- Aisle lights ON.
- Step lights ON.
- Screen dim.
- Seats clearly visible.

The user must be able to see the auditorium properly.

## STATE 2 — SHOW STARTING

Before the first video:

- Begin a smooth cinematic transition.
- Gradually reduce ceiling light intensity.
- Fade out main lights.
- Reduce wall lights.
- Increase screen brightness.
- Keep step lighting visible.

## STATE 3 — SHOW PLAYING

While videos are playing:

- Main lights OFF.
- Ceiling lights OFF.
- Wall lights OFF or nearly OFF.
- Aisle lighting very dim.
- Step lights very dim.
- Screen ON.
- Screen light softly illuminates nearby seats and walls.

Step lighting must be subtle.

Do not make the auditorium completely black.

The remaining lights must not disturb the screen viewing experience.

## STATE 4 — SHOW COMPLETED

After the final video:

- Fade out the screen.
- Stop the playlist.
- Gradually turn on aisle lights.
- Turn on wall lights.
- Turn on ceiling lights.
- Turn on main lights.
- Restore the complete auditorium lighting.

Use smooth transitions.

Do not use instant on/off switching.

Lighting profiles must be configurable for every theater category.

